\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Set 6: Models of Qualitative Responses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract

\series bold
\size normal
Models of Limited Dependent Variables:
\series default
 Binary Choice Model; Multiple Choice Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider a model where a dependent variable (latent variable) 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 has mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{t}$
\end_inset

, which is a linear function of a set of exogenous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}^{*}=E(y_{t}^{*})=u_{t}=X_{t}\beta+u_{t},\ t=1,\ldots,N
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}\beta$
\end_inset

 is called an 
\emph on
index function
\emph default
.
 The latent variable model is often motivated by the maximization of 
\emph on
random utilities
\emph default
 of two choices.
 Let the utility of choice 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 be 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{ij}=Z_{ij}\beta+\epsilon_{ij},\ j=0,1$
\end_inset

, for individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 He will choose alternative 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{i1}>U_{i0}$
\end_inset

, that is, if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}^{*}\equiv U_{i1}-U_{i0}=(Z_{i1}-Z_{i0})\beta+(\epsilon_{i1}-\epsilon_{i0})\equiv X_{i}\beta+u_{i}>0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Models of limited dependent variables are the cases in which observations
 on the dependent variable are not fully available.
 In the binary response (choice) model, only the signs of the dependent
 variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 are observed while observations on regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 are fully available.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 be the observable random variable.
 Then, we can specify the 
\emph on
binary response model
\emph default
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}^{*}>0\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}^{*}\leq0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When there are more than two choices, two cases arise: 
\emph on
ordered
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
unordered 
\emph default
qualitative response (choice) models.
 A classical example of ordered qualitative response model (OQR) is a survey
 data where the answers are to choose one from strongly agree, agree, indifferen
t, disagree, and strongly disagree.
 This can be considered as a direct extension of the binary choice model.
 A classical example of the unordered qualitative response model (UQR) is
 the choice of a transportation mode among driving, riding on a bus, walking,
 etc.
 The ordered and unordered qualitative response models are also called the
 ordered and unordered multinomial models.
 We will discuss these models after the binary response model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Binary Response Model (BRM)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Specification of the Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the BRM, we may directly define the observed values 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 as the realizations of a Bernoulli random variable.
 The probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{t}=1)$
\end_inset

 is assume to be a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}\beta$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P(y_{t}=1) & = & F(X_{t}\beta)\equiv F_{t}\\
P(y_{t}=0) & = & 1-F(X_{t}\beta)\equiv1-F_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

This interpretation of the BRM required that 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is a monotonic nondecreasing function with 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-\infty)=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\infty)=1$
\end_inset

.
 A cumulative distribution function (cdf) satisfies these conditions.
 The function 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is sometimes called a 
\emph on
transformation function
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can derive the same result from the latent variable model 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}=X_{t}\beta+u_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $F(u_{t})$
\end_inset

 be the cdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
P(y_{t}=1)=P(y_{t}^{*}>0)=1-P(y_{t}^{*}<0)=1-P(u_{t}<-X_{t}\beta)=1-F(-X_{t}\beta)\\
P(y_{t}=0)=1-P(y_{t}=1)=F(-X_{t}\beta)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

If the distribution function is symmetric, then 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-X_{t}\beta)=1-F(X_{t}\beta)$
\end_inset

, and hence we have the same results as the direct specification of the
 BRM.
 We will assume a symmetric distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 when the model refers to the latent variable interpretation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Intepretation of the Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Non-constant Marginal Effects of Regressors on Probability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A primary interest in a regression model is the marginal effects of the
 regressors on the (conditional) mean of the dependent variable.
 In the BRM, it is the marginal effects on the probability of choice 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{t}=1)$
\end_inset

 that is of more interest.
 Of course, in the Bernoulli binary choice specification, this probability
 is the same as the mean of the dependent variable.
 Since the effect of a regressor on 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{t}=1)$
\end_inset

 is a nonlinear function in general, we need to compute it as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial E(y_{t})}{\partial X_{tk}}=\frac{\partial P(y_{t}=1)}{\partial X_{tk}}=\frac{\partial F(X_{t}\beta)}{\partial X_{tk}}=f(X_{t}\beta)\beta_{k}
\]

\end_inset

when 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{tk}$
\end_inset

 is a continuous variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Evaluations of the Marginal Effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Noe that this marginal effect depends on the values of all regressors.
 Typically, this marginal effect is evaluated at the sample mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset

 of the regressors.
 However, it is very informative to compute this effect for several sets
 of the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 For example, holding the vlaues of other regressors at their sample means,
 one may compute the marginal effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{tk}$
\end_inset

 for a wide range of the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{tk}$
\end_inset

.
 When the regressor 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{tk}$
\end_inset

 of interest is a discrete variable (dummy variable) taking values 0 or
 1, the derivative is not a proper measure.
 A more proper measure is the difference in 
\begin_inset Formula $F(X_{t}\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

 beween 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{tk}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{tk}=0$
\end_inset

, holding all other continuous regressors at a fixed value (their means).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Effect of the Shapte of the Distribution Funciton
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The marginal effect also depends on the density function.
 One may get a very different profile of the marginal effect, depending
 on the choice of the transformation function.
 For example, suppose we wish to compare the marginal effect of income on
 the probability of making a charitable contribution.
 Suppose we used the standard normal cdf for 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 to estimate the model, and found 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{H}\hat{\beta}>X_{L}\hat{\beta}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(X_{H}\hat{\beta})<f(X_{L}\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

, which implies a larger marginal effect for the low income individual.
 This is mainly due to the choice of the model density function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Other Quantities of Interest
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the marginal effect of the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

th regressor 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{k}$
\end_inset

 changes over time (or across individuals) as its value changes, we may
 take the sample average of the marginal effects as a measure of the marginal
 effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{k}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{estimate of}\ \frac{\partial P(y_{t}=1)}{\partial X_{k}}=\frac{1}{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}f(X_{t}\hat{\beta})\hat{\beta}=\bar{\hat{f}}\hat{\beta}_{k}
\]

\end_inset

Another measure of interest is the marginal effect of a regressor on the
 
\emph on
odds ratio
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
log odds ratio
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
q_{t}=\frac{P(y_{t}=1)}{P(y_{t}=0)}=\frac{F(X_{t}\beta)}{1-F(X_{t}\beta)}\\
\ln q_{t}=\ln\left[F(X_{t}\beta)\right]-\ln\left[1-F(X_{t}\beta)\right]
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

The marginal effects are easily computed from
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{dq_{t}}{dX_{tk}}=\frac{f_{t}}{(1-F_{t})^{2}}\beta_{k}\\
\frac{d\ln q_{t}}{dX_{tk}}=\frac{f_{t}}{F_{t}(1-F_{t})}\beta_{k}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
A Measurement of Goodness-of-Fit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

How well does the model explain the binary decision? A standard way to measure
 it is to compare the actual choice and predicted choice, where the predicted
 choice is determined by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{t}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $F(X_{t}\hat{\beta})>c$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $F(X_{t}\hat{\beta})\leq c$
\end_inset

 for some constant 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

, typically 0.5.
 One may compute the correct number of predicitons as a measure of the goodness
 of fit.
 The choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $c=0.5$
\end_inset

 seems to be reasonable, but somewhat arbitrary.
 An alternative method may use the confidence intervals of 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}=F(X_{t}\beta)$
\end_inset

 based on their estimated variances.
 If 95% confidence interval includes 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}=1$
\end_inset

, then we take the predicted event as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{t}=1$
\end_inset

, etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Estimation of the Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Least Squares Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The most widely used estimation method of the binary choice model is the
 MLE.
 Before we consider it, we will consider the least squares estimator of
 the coefficient vector in
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=X_{t}\beta+u_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where we simply use the binary values of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=0,1$
\end_inset

 as the observations of the dependent variable.
 For this specificaiton to satisfy the assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t})=0$
\end_inset

, it requires
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(u_{t}) & = & E(y_{t}-X_{t}\beta)=0\\
 & = & (1-X_{t}\beta)F_{t}-X_{t}\beta(1-F_{t})=0\\
 & \implies & F_{t}=X_{t}\beta
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and hence
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(u_{t}^{2}) & = & \left[1-2X_{t}\beta+(X_{t}\beta)^{2}\right]F_{t}+(X_{t}\beta)^{2}(1-F_{t})\\
 & = & F_{t}-2F_{t}+F_{t}^{2}\\
 & = & F_{t}(1-F_{t})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

That is, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 is heteroskedastic if it has zero mean.
 Therefore, the coefficient will be estimated by the GLS, which is identival
 to the MLE under the assumption of the standard uniform distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Maximum Likelihood Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The density function of a Bernoulli random variable 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P(Y_{t}=1)=F_{t}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(Y_{t}=y_{t})=F_{t}^{y_{t}}(1-F_{t})^{1-y_{t}},\ y_{t}=0\ \mbox{or}\ 1
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, the likelihood function 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 and the log-likelihood function 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 of an 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 independent sample 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t},\ t=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

 are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & {\displaystyle \prod_{t=1}^{N}}F_{t}^{y_{t}}(1-F_{t})^{1-y_{t}}\\
l & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}y_{t}\ln F_{t}+{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}(1-y_{t})\ln(1-F_{t})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

It is straight forward to derive the gradient vector and Hessian matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To find the gradient, note first that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{dF_{t}}{d\beta}=\frac{dF(X_{t}\beta)}{d\beta}=f_{t}X_{t}^{\prime},\ f_{t}=f(X_{t}\beta)
\]

\end_inset

Therefore the gradient vector is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
g(\beta)\equiv\frac{\partial l}{\partial\beta} & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\left[y_{t}\frac{f_{t}}{F_{t}}-(1-y_{t})\frac{f_{t}}{1-F_{t}}\right]X_{t}^{\prime}\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\left[\frac{y_{t}-y_{t}F_{t}-F_{t}+y_{t}F_{t}}{F_{t}(1-F_{t})}\right]f_{t}X_{t}^{\prime}\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\frac{y_{t}-F_{t}}{F_{t}(1-F_{t})}f_{t}X_{t}^{\prime}\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\left(f_{t}X_{t}\right)^{\prime}\left[F_{t}\left(1-F_{t}\right)\right]^{-1}\left(y_{t}-F_{t}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To find the Hessian matrix, consider
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial}{\partial\beta^{\prime}}\left[\frac{y_{t}-F_{t}}{F_{t}\left(1-F_{t}\right)}f_{t}\right]=\frac{y_{t}-F_{t}}{F_{t}\left(1-F_{t}\right)}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{t}(z)}{\partial z}X_{t}+D_{2}f_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial f(z)}{\partial z}$
\end_inset

 is the derivative of the density function with respect to its argument,
 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
D_{2} & = & \frac{1}{\left[F_{t}\left(1-F_{t}\right)\right]^{2}}\left(-F_{t}(1-F_{t})f_{t}X_{t}-(y_{t}-F_{t})(1-2F_{t})f_{t}X_{t}\right)\\
 & = & \frac{1}{\left[F_{t}\left(1-F_{t}\right)\right]^{2}}\left(-F_{t}f_{t}X_{t}+F_{t}^{2}f_{t}X_{t}-y_{t}f_{t}X_{t}+y_{t}2F_{t}f_{t}X_{t}+F_{t}f_{t}X_{t}-2F_{t}^{2}f_{t}X_{t}\right)\\
 & = & -\frac{f_{t}X_{t}}{\left[F_{t}\left(1-F_{t}\right)\right]^{2}}\left[F_{t}^{2}+y_{t}\left(1-2F_{t}\right)\right]\\
 & = & -\frac{f_{t}X_{t}}{\left[F_{t}\left(1-F_{t}\right)\right]^{2}}\left[y_{t}-F_{t}\right]^{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where the last equality is due to the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=y_{t}^{2}$
\end_inset

 for the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=1$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the Hessian matrix is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
H & = & \frac{\partial^{2}l}{\partial\beta\partial\beta^{\prime}}\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\frac{y_{t}-F_{t}}{F_{t}(1-F_{t})}\frac{\partial f_{t}}{\partial\beta^{\prime}}X_{t}^{\prime}X_{t}-{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\frac{f_{t}^{2}X_{t}^{\prime}X_{t}}{\left[F_{t}\left(1-F_{t}\right)\right]^{2}}\left[y_{t}-F_{t}\right]^{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The MLE 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 as the root of the gradient vector 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\hat{\beta})=0$
\end_inset

 has an asymptotic normal distribution.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{a}\\
\sim
\end{array}N(0,\Psi^{-1})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi=E\left(-N^{-1}H\right)$
\end_inset

 is the information matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
EM Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An alternative procedure is the 
\series bold
\emph on
EM algorithm
\series default
\emph default
, which fills the missing observations with its conditional expectiation.
 The likelihood function for the MLE is written in terms of the observed
 variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The EM (expectation-maximization) algorithm writes the likelihood function
 in terms of the latent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 conditional on observed variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 and initial estimate of parameters
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
l={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\ln f(y_{t}^{*}|y_{t},X_{t},\beta)
\]

\end_inset

The first (expectation) step of the EM algortihm is to take the expected
 value of this log-likelihood function, and the second maximizaiton step
 is to find a new estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 that maximizes the expected likelihood
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{\beta}}\ E\left({\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\ln f(y_{t}^{*}|y_{t},X_{t},\beta)\right)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the case of BRM, this procedure is equivalent to replacing the missing
 observations 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 with its expected value conditional on observed value 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 That is replace 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=1$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{t}^{*}|y_{t}^{*}>0$
\end_inset

, and replace 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{t}^{*}|y_{t}^{*}\leq0)$
\end_inset

.
 From the latent variable model we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
E(y_{t}^{*}|y_{t}^{*}>0)\equiv E(y_{t}^{*}|u_{t}>-X_{t}\beta)=X_{t}\beta+E(u_{t}|u_{t}>-X_{t}\beta)\\
E(y_{t}^{*}|y_{t}^{*}\leq0)\equiv E(y_{t}^{*}|u_{t}\leq-X_{t}\beta)=X_{t}\beta+E(u_{t}|u_{t}\leq-X_{t}\beta)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

The conditional means are derived from the 
\series bold
\emph on
truncated distributions
\series default
\emph default
, which are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
f(u_{t}|u_{t}>-X_{t}\beta)=\begin{cases}
\frac{f(u_{t})}{1-F(-X_{t}\beta)} & \mbox{if}\ u_{t}>-X_{t}\beta\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}\\
f(u_{t}|u_{t}\leq-X_{t}\beta)=\begin{cases}
\frac{f(u_{t})}{F(-X_{t}\beta)} & \mbox{if}\ u_{t}\leq-X_{t}\beta\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Since both expected values are functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, evaluate each by the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

th iterative value 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, use these as the dependent variable and apply OLS against the regressors
 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 to obtain a new estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{i+1}$
\end_inset

.
 Repeat this until converegence is obtained.
 The EM estimator converges to the MLE.
 The drawback of this EM algorithm is that the asymptotic covariance matrix
 is not a part of the computation procedure and has to be compute separately.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Distribution of the Estimators of Probability and Marginal Effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Once the estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is compute, we can compute the predicted value of the probability and the
 marginal effects of regressors on the probability,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\hat{F}_{t}=F(X_{t}\hat{\beta})\\
\hat{m}_{t}\equiv\frac{\partial F(X_{t}\hat{\beta})}{\partial X_{t}^{\prime}}=f(X_{t}\hat{\beta})\hat{\beta}=\hat{f}_{t}\hat{\beta}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

which are nonlinear functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 The variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{F}_{t}$
\end_inset

 and the covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{m}_{t}$
\end_inset

 can be found by applying a linear approximation approach (
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

-method), which gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\hat{F}_{t}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{a}\\
\sim
\end{array}N(F_{t},f_{t}^{2}X_{t}VX_{t}^{\prime}),\ V=\mbox{cov}(\hat{\beta})\\
\hat{m}_{t}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{a}\\
\sim
\end{array}N\left(m_{t},(f_{t}I_{K}+d_{t}\beta X_{t})V(f_{t}I_{K}+d_{t}\beta X_{t})^{\prime}\right)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{t}=\frac{\partial f_{t}}{\partial(X_{t}\beta)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Choice of the Transformation Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The parametric approach to the estimation of the model chooses a parametric
 distribution (transformation) function.
 Most commonly uses functions are normal and logistic:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Probit Model:
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}\sim N(0,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
F(Z)=\Phi\left(\nicefrac{z}{\sigma}\right)\\
f(z)=\left(\nicefrac{1}{\sigma}\right)\cdot\phi\left(\nicefrac{z}{\sigma}\right)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 are the standard normal cdf and pdf, respectively.
 Parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 are not identifiable and hence normalization restriction 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma=1$
\end_inset

 is usually imposed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Logit Model
\emph default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}\sim Logistic(0,\nicefrac{\pi^{2}}{3})$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
F(z)=\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}=\frac{e^{z}}{1+e^{z}}\\
f(z)=\frac{e^{z}}{(1+e^{z})^{2}}=F(z)F(-z)=F(z)[1-F(z)]
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

A more general logistic cdf is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
F(z)=\frac{1}{1+\exp(\nicefrac{-(z-a)}{b})},\ -\infty<a<\infty,\ b>0\\
E(Z)=a,\ \mbox{Var}(Z)=\frac{(b\pi)^{2}}{3}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\emph on
Linear Probability Model
\emph default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}\sim U(0,1)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(z)=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if}\ z\leq0\\
z & \mbox{if}\ 0<z\leq1\\
1 & \mbox{if}\ z>1
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\emph on
Weibull Model
\emph default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}\sim Weibull(\lambda,\alpha)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
F(z)=1-\exp(-\lambda z^{\alpha})\\
f(z)=\lambda\alpha z^{\alpha-1}\exp(-\lambda z^{\alpha})
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

This is an asymmetric density.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Classical Tests of Nested Hypothesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Tests of nest hypothesis about the linear or nonlinear restrictions on the
 coefficient vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 can be conducted by using one of the three classical test methods: LR,
 Wald, and LM tests.
 Let the hypothesis to be tested by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H_{0}:r(\beta)=q
\]

\end_inset

against
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H_{1}:r(\beta)\neq q
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r(\beta)$
\end_inset

 is a set of 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 linear or nonlinear equations of 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $J\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of known constanst.
 Let
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
R(\beta)=\left(\frac{\partial r(\beta)}{\partial\beta}\right)^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

be the set of partial derivatives of dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $J\times K$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $r(\beta)$
\end_inset

 is a linear function 
\begin_inset Formula $r(\beta)=R(\beta)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 does not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be the unrestricted and restricted MLEs, respectively.
 Then, the classical test statistics which have the asymptotic null distribution
 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}(J)$
\end_inset

 are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
LRT=2\left[l(\hat{\beta})-l(\tilde{\beta})\right]\\
WT=\left(r(\beta)-q\right)^{\prime}\left[R(\hat{\beta})V(\hat{\beta})R(\hat{\beta})\right]^{-1}\left(r(\beta)-q\right)\\
LMT=g(\tilde{\beta})^{\prime}V(\tilde{\beta})g(\tilde{\beta})
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $V(\hat{\beta})=\left[-EH(\beta)\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Ordered Qualitative Response Models (OQR)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The OQR model can be considered in terms of the latent variable model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}^{*}=X_{t}\beta+u_{t},\ t=1,\ldots,N
\]

\end_inset

and the observed variables are choices among three alternatives (say)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}^{*}<\alpha_{1}\\
1 & \mbox{if}\ \alpha_{1}\leq y_{t}^{*}<\alpha_{2}\\
2 & \mbox{if}\ \alpha_{2}\leq y_{t}^{*}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

As in the binary choice model, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 can be set to zero without loss of generality if 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 has a constant intercept term.
 The probability of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=j$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
P(y_{t}=0)=P(y_{t}^{*}<\alpha_{1})=P(u_{t}<\alpha_{1}-X_{t}\beta)=F(\alpha_{1}-X_{t}\beta)\\
P(y_{t}=1)=P(\alpha_{1}\leq y_{t}^{*}<\alpha_{2})=P(\alpha_{1}-X_{t}\beta\leq u_{t}<\alpha_{2}-X_{t}\beta)=F(\alpha_{2}-X_{t}\beta)-F(\alpha_{1}-X_{t}\beta)\\
P(y_{t}=2)=P(y_{t}^{*}\geq\alpha_{1})=P(u_{t}\geq\alpha_{2}-X_{t}\beta)=1-F(\alpha_{2}-X_{t}\beta)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Therefore, the log likelihood function can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
l(\beta,\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2})={\displaystyle \sum_{y_{t}=0}}\ln F(\alpha_{1}-X_{t}\beta)+{\displaystyle \sum_{y_{t}=1}}\ln\left[F(\alpha_{2}-X_{t}\beta)-F(\alpha_{1}-X_{t}\beta)\right]+{\displaystyle \sum_{y_{t}=2}}\ln\left[1-F(\alpha_{2}-X_{t}\beta)\right]
\]

\end_inset

More generally, let the probabilities of 
\begin_inset Formula $J+1$
\end_inset

 choices be
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{tj}\equiv P(y_{t}=j)=P(\alpha_{j}\leq y_{t}^{*}<\alpha_{j+1})=F(\alpha_{j+1}-X_{t}\beta)-F(\alpha_{j}-X_{t}\beta),\ j=0,\ldots,J
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{0}=-\infty$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{J+1}=\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
l(\beta,\alpha)={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{j=0}^{J}}y_{tj}\ln\left[F(\alpha_{j+1}-X_{t}\beta)-F(\alpha_{j}-X_{t}\beta)\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{tj}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

th choice is selected and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{tj}=0$
\end_inset

 otherwise.
 The marginal effects of the regressors on the probabilities 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{tj}$
\end_inset

 are easily computed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The marginal effects of regressors on each probability can be computed as
 before.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial p_{tj}}{\partial X_{t}^{\prime}}=\left[f(\alpha_{j+1}-X_{t}\beta)-f(\alpha_{j}-X_{t}\beta)\right]\beta,\ j=0,\ldots,J
\]

\end_inset

The sign of this marginal effect depends not only on the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{k}$
\end_inset

, but also on the sign difference between the two values of the density.
 Thus, it is difficult to interpret the marginal effects of regressors on
 the probabilities.
 Of course, the sign and magnitude of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{k}$
\end_inset

 represent its effect on the latent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Unordered Qualitative Response Models (UQR)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An important point in the ordered qualitative response model is that it
 has a single index function 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}\beta$
\end_inset

, that is, it is common to all choices and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 does not have characteristics of a particular choice.
 In unordered qualitative response models, the regressors may vary across
 choices.
 The unordered choice model is often based on an additive random utility
 model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let the random utility of alternative 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 for an individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 be specified as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{ij}=W_{ij}\delta_{j}+\epsilon_{ij}=m_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij},\ =1,\ldots,J
\]

\end_inset

There are two types of regressors: regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 that are individual specific and common to all alternatives, and regressors
 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{ij}$
\end_inset

 that are alternative-specific and represent the attributes of the choices.
 The income of individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is an example of the former, and an example of the latter is the prices
 of alternative transportation modes.
 When both types of regressors are present, we can specify the random utility
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{ij}=X_{i}\beta_{j}+Z_{ij}\gamma+\epsilon_{ij}=m_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij},\ j=1,2,\ldots,J
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that individual-specific regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 are assumed to have different marginal effects on utility and alternative-speci
fic regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{ij}$
\end_inset

 are assumed to have the same marginal effects on utilities.
 The model is called the 
\emph on
multinomial model 
\emph default
if only individual-specific regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 are included 
\begin_inset Formula $(\gamma=0)$
\end_inset

, and it is called the 
\emph on
conditional model
\emph default
 if only choice-specific regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{ij}$
\end_inset

 are included 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta_{j}=0)$
\end_inset

.
 When both type of regressors are present, it is sometimes called the 
\emph on
mixed model
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The multinomial model or the mixed model can be expressed as a conditional
 model.
 Consider an example of 3 alternatives.
 Write the mean function in matrix form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{c}
m_{i1}\\
m_{i2}\\
m_{i3}
\end{array}\right]=X_{i}\left[\begin{array}{c}
\beta_{i1}\\
\beta_{i2}\\
\beta_{3}
\end{array}\right]+\left[\begin{array}{c}
Z_{i1}\\
Z_{i2}\\
Z_{i3}
\end{array}\right]\gamma=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
X_{i} & 0 & 0\\
0 & X_{i} & 0\\
0 & 0 & X_{i}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\beta_{i1}\\
\beta_{i2}\\
\beta_{i3}
\end{array}\right]+\left[\begin{array}{c}
Z_{i1}\\
Z_{i2}\\
Z_{i3}
\end{array}\right]\gamma=\left[\begin{array}{c}
X_{i1}\\
X_{i2}\\
X_{i3}
\end{array}\right]\beta+\left[\begin{array}{c}
Z_{i1}\\
Z_{i2}\\
Z_{i3}
\end{array}\right]\gamma
\]

\end_inset

which can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{ij}=X_{ij}\beta+Z_{ij}\gamma$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{ij}=D_{j}^{\prime}\otimes X_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a column vector with zeros except for the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

th position.
 In this expression, both regressors vary across alternatives and both coefficie
nt vectors are constant, the characteristics of the conditional model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 will choose alternative 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{ij}>U_{ik}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq j$
\end_inset

.
 When alternative 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is chosen, we observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{ij}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{ik}=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq j$
\end_inset

.
 We wish to specify the probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{ij}=1)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P(y_{ij}=1) & = & P\left[U_{ij}=\max\left(U_{i1},\ldots,U_{iJ}\right)\right]\\
 & = & P\left(m_{ik}+\epsilon_{ik}<m_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij},\ \forall\ k\neq j\right)\\
 & = & P\left(\epsilon_{ik}<(m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij},\ \forall\ k\neq j\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

This probability can be found by first considered the conditional probability
 for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset

, and then compute the weighted average of the conditional probability with
 weights equal to the probability of 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{ij}=1)={\displaystyle \int}P\left(\epsilon_{ik}<(m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij}|\epsilon_{ij};\forall\ k\neq j\right)f(\epsilon_{ij})\ d\epsilon_{ij}
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset

 are i.i.d.
 random variables with a cdf 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\epsilon_{ij})$
\end_inset

, then the conditional probability is the same as the marginal probability
 and the joint probability is the product of marginal probabilities.
 Hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P\left(\epsilon_{ik}<(m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij}|\epsilon_{ij};\forall\ k\neq j\right)={\displaystyle \prod_{k\neq j}}F\left((m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij}\right)
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{ij}\equiv P(y_{ij}=1)={\displaystyle \int}\left[{\displaystyle \prod_{k\neq j}}F\left((m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij}\right)\right]f(\epsilon_{ij})\ d\epsilon_{ij}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Choice of Distribution Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The most widely used distribution function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is the standard extreme value (maximum) distribution of Type I, which is
 also called the 
\emph on
Gumbel distribution
\emph default
.
 It has the pdf and cdf of the following forms
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
f(z)=\exp(-z)\cdot\exp(-e^{-z})=e^{-z}F(z)\\
F(z)=\exp(-e^{-z})
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

The model with this distribution is called the 
\emph on
multinomial logit model
\emph default
 if only individual-specific regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 are included, and it is called the 
\emph on
conditional logit model
\emph default
 if only choice-specific regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{ij}$
\end_inset

 are included.
 When both types of regressors are present, it is sometimes called the 
\emph on
mixed logit model
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset

 are i.i.d.
 standard Gumbel (maximum random variables, the integrand in the computation
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ij}$
\end_inset

 can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{ij}=P(y_{ij}=1)={\displaystyle \int}\Psi\ d\epsilon_{ij}
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\Psi & = & \left[{\displaystyle \prod_{k\neq j}}F\left((m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij}\right)\right]f(\epsilon_{ij})\\
 & = & \left[{\displaystyle \prod_{k\neq j}}\exp\left(-e^{-(m_{ij}-m_{ik}+\epsilon_{ij})}\right)\right]e^{-\epsilon_{ij}}\exp\left(-e^{-\epsilon_{ij}}\right)\\
 & = & \exp\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq j}}e^{-(m_{ij}-m_{ik})-\epsilon_{ij}}\right]e^{-\epsilon_{ij}}\exp\left(-e^{-\epsilon_{ij}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau={\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq j}}e^{-(m_{ij}-m_{ik})}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w=\epsilon_{ij}-\ln(1+\tau)$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\tau=\exp(\ln(1+\tau))$
\end_inset

.
 Now,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\ln\Psi & = & -e^{-\epsilon_{ij}}{\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq j}}e^{-(m_{ij}-m_{ik})}-\epsilon_{ij}-e^{-\epsilon_{ij}}\\
 & = & -\epsilon_{ij}-e^{-\epsilon_{ij}}\left[1+{\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq j}}e^{-(m_{ij}-m_{ik})}\right]\\
 & = & -\epsilon_{ij}-e^{-\epsilon_{ij}+\ln(1+\tau)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Taking the exponent on both sides yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\Psi & = & \exp(-\epsilon_{ij})\exp\left(-e^{-\epsilon_{ij}+\ln(1+\tau)}\right)\\
 & = & \frac{e^{\ln(1+\tau)}}{1+\tau}\exp(-\epsilon_{ij})\exp\left(-e^{-\epsilon_{ij}+\ln(1+\tau)}\right)\\
 & = & \frac{\exp\left(-\epsilon_{ij}+\ln(1+\tau)\right)}{1+\tau}\exp\left(-e^{-\epsilon_{ij}+\ln(1+\tau)}\right)\\
 & = & \frac{e^{-w}}{1+\tau}\exp\left(-e^{-w}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P(y_{ij}=1) & = & {\displaystyle \int}\Psi\ d\epsilon_{ij}\\
 & = & \frac{1}{1+\tau}{\displaystyle \int}e^{-w}\exp(e^{-w})\ dw\ \ \mbox{since}\ dw=d\epsilon_{ij}\\
 & = & \frac{1}{1+\tau}{\displaystyle \int}f(w)\ dw\\
 & = & \frac{1}{1+\tau}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Finally,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1+\tau & = & 1+{\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq j}}e^{-(m_{ij}-m_{ik})}\\
 & = & 1+e^{-m_{ij}}{\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq j}}e^{m_{ik}}\\
 & = & e^{-m_{ij}}\left(e^{m_{ij}}+{\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq j}}e^{m_{ik}}\right)\\
 & = & e^{-m_{ij}}{\displaystyle \sum_{k}}e^{m_{ik}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{ij}=1)=\frac{e^{m_{ij}}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}e^{m_{ik}}}=\frac{e^{X_{i}\beta_{j}}e^{Z_{ij}\gamma}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}e^{X_{i}\beta_{k}}e^{Z_{ik}\gamma}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Identification of Parameters in Logit Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Parameters of the individual-specific regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 are not all identifiable.
 This means that two coefficient vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset

 give an identical probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ij}=P(y_{ij}=1)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}_{j}=\beta_{j}+\alpha$
\end_inset

 for a constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 To see this, consider the mean function 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{m}_{ij}=X_{i}\tilde{\beta}_{j}+Z_{ij}\gamma=X_{i}\beta_{j}+Z_{ij}\gamma+X_{i}\alpha=m_{ij}+X_{i}\alpha
\]

\end_inset

Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\exp(\tilde{m}_{ij})=\exp(m_{ij})\exp(X_{i}\alpha)
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}\exp(\tilde{m}_{ik})=\exp(X_{i}\alpha){\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}\exp(m_{ik})
\]

\end_inset

So,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{p}_{ij}=\frac{\exp(\tilde{m}_{ij})}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}\exp(\tilde{m}_{ik})}=\frac{\exp(m_{ij})}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}\exp(m_{ik})}=p_{ij}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset

 lead to the identical probability.
 To identify the parameters in this model, an identification restriction
 is imposed usually in the form of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Under this restriction, the choice probabilities become
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{i1}=\frac{\exp(Z_{i1}\gamma)}{\exp(Z_{i1}\gamma)+{\displaystyle \sum_{k=2}^{J}}\exp(X_{i}\beta_{k})\exp(Z_{ik}\gamma)}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{ij}=\frac{\exp(X_{i}\beta_{j})\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma)}{\exp(Z_{i1}\gamma)+{\displaystyle \sum_{k=2}^{J}}\exp(X_{i}\beta_{k})\exp(Z_{ik}\gamma)},\ j\geq2
\]

\end_inset

In the case of the 
\emph on
multinomial logit model
\emph default
 (only regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=0$
\end_inset

), the choice probabilities become
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{i1}=\frac{1}{1+{\displaystyle \sum_{k=2}^{J}}\exp(X_{i}\beta_{k})}\equiv\frac{1}{D}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{ij}=\frac{\exp(X_{i}\beta_{j})}{1+{\displaystyle \sum_{k=2}^{J}}\exp(X_{i}\beta_{k})}=\frac{\exp(X_{i}\beta_{j})}{D},\ j\geq2
\]

\end_inset

For the 
\emph on
conditional logit model
\emph default
 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}=0$
\end_inset

), the probabilities are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{ij}=\frac{\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma)}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}\exp(Z_{ik}\gamma)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
MLE of Multinomial Logit Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The log likelihood function for all observations is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & \ln\left[{\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \prod_{j=1}^{J}}p_{ij}^{y_{ij}}\right]\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}y_{ij}\ln p_{ij}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ij}$
\end_inset

 of the multinomial logit model, the log likelihood function becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & \sum_{i=1}^{n}\left[-y_{i1}\ln(D)+{\displaystyle \sum_{j=2}^{J}}y_{ij}\left(X_{i}\beta_{j}-\ln(D)\right)\right]\\
 & = & \sum_{i=1}^{n}\left[-\ln(D){\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}y_{ij}+{\displaystyle \sum_{j=2}^{J}}y_{ij}X_{i}\beta_{j}-\ln(D)\right]\\
 & = & \sum_{i=1}^{n}\left[-\ln(D)+{\displaystyle \sum_{j=2}^{J}}y_{ij}X_{i}\beta_{j}-\ln(D)\right]\ \ \mbox{since}\ {\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}y_{ij}\\
L & \equiv & {\displaystyle \sum_{=1}^{n}}L_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

It is straightforward to derive the gradient vectors
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\beta_{j}}={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}(y_{ij}-p_{ij})X_{i}^{\prime},\ j\geq2
\]

\end_inset

the diagonal elements of the Hessian matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial^{2}L}{\partial\beta_{j}\partial\beta_{j}^{\prime}}=-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p_{ij}(1-p_{ij})X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i},\ j\geq2
\]

\end_inset

and the off-diagonal blocks
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial^{2}L}{\partial\beta_{j}\partial\beta_{s}^{\prime}}=-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p_{ij}p_{is}X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i},\ j\geq2
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Interpretation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that the odds ratio
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{p_{ij}}{p_{i1}}=\exp(X_{i}\beta_{j})
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, the log of the odds ratio against the probability of the normalized
 parameter becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln\left(\frac{p_{ij}}{p_{i1}}\right)=X_{i}\beta_{j}
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, the coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 represents the marginal effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 on the logs odd ratio.
 Similarly, the log odds ratio
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln\left(\frac{p_{ij}}{p_{is}}\right)=X_{i}(\beta_{j}-\beta_{s}),\ j\neq s\geq2
\]

\end_inset

indicates that the differences in coefficients represent the marginal effects
 of regressors on the difference in log probabilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The marginal effects of the regressors on the probability of a particular
 alternative depend on all values of coefficient vectors.
 To see this for the case of continuous regressors, take the derivative
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial p_{ij}}{X_{i}^{\prime}}=\frac{1}{D^{2}}\left[D\exp(X_{i}\beta_{j})\beta_{j}-\exp(X_{i}\beta_{j}){\displaystyle \sum_{k=2}^{J}}\exp(X_{i}\beta_{k})\beta_{k}\right]=p_{ij}\beta_{j}-p_{ij}{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}p_{ik}\beta_{k}=p_{ij}(\beta_{j}-\bar{\beta})
\]

\end_inset

where we used the normalization 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is the weighted mean (expected value) of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the marginal effect of a particular regressor 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{ik}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is positive if and only if its coefficient for the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

th alternative is greater than the average of its coefficients for all althernat
ives 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta_{jk}>\bar{\beta}_{k})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
MLE of Conditional Logit Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The probabilities of the conditional logit model are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{ij}=\frac{\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma)}{D_{c}}
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{c}={\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma),\ j=1,\ldots,J
\]

\end_inset

The log likelihood function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}y_{ij}\ln(p_{ij})={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}y_{ij}\left(Z_{ij}\gamma-\ln(D_{c})\right)
\]

\end_inset

It is easy to show the gradient vector
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\gamma}={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}y_{ij}(Z_{ij}^{\prime}-\bar{Z}_{i}^{\prime}),\ \bar{Z}_{i}={\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}p_{ik}Z_{ik}^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

and Hessian matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial^{2}L}{\partial\gamma\partial\gamma^{\prime}}=-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}p_{ik}\left(Z_{ik}-\bar{Z}_{i}\right)^{\prime}\left(Z_{ik}-\bar{Z}_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

The log odds ratio between two alternatives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln\left(\frac{p_{ij}}{p_{ik}}\right)=\left(Z_{ij}-Z_{ik}\right)\gamma,\ j,k=1,\ldots,J
\]

\end_inset

indicates that it depends only on the attributes of alternatives in comparison.
 This property of the conditional logit model is called the 
\emph on
independence of irrelevant alternatives
\emph default
.
 This property simplifies the estimation problem, but it is an undesirable
 property from the view point of economic behavior of economic agents.
 Consider the classical example of three choices, traveling in a car, a
 red bus and a blue bus.
 The relative probability between the car and the red bus is most likely
 to change when the alternative blue bus is introduced.
 But, the 
\emph on
independence of irrelevant alternatives
\emph default
 indicates that the relative probability between the car and the red bus
 will not be affected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One of the reasons for this property is the assumption of the independence
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 The independence implies that the conditional probability of choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 given that either 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 alternatives are chosen is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{ij}=1|y_{ij}=1\ \mbox{or}\ y_{ik}=1)=\frac{p_{ij}}{p_{ij}+p_{ik}}=\frac{\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma)}{\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma)+\exp(Z_{ik}\gamma)}
\]

\end_inset

which does not depend on attributes of other alternatives except for those
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 alternatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The marginal effects of the regressors on the probability of a particular
 alternative depends on all values of coefficient vectors.
 To see this for the case of continuous regressors, take the derivative.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{\partial p_{ij}}{\partial Z_{ij}}=\frac{1}{D_{c}^{2}}\left(D_{c}\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma)\gamma^{\prime}-\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma)\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma)\gamma^{\prime}\right)=p_{ij}(1-p_{ij})\gamma^{\prime}\\
\frac{\partial p_{ij}}{\partial Z_{is}}=\frac{1}{D_{c}^{2}}\left(0-\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma)\exp(Z_{ij}\gamma)\gamma^{\prime}\right)=-p_{ij}p_{is}\gamma^{\prime},\ s\neq j
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

The marginal effect of an alternative-specific attribute on its own probability
 has the same sign as the sign of the coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

, while the cross marginal effect has a sign opposite to the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

